            ("The predictability horizon", "The time that a forecast stays useful is approximately (1/λ) ln(Δ/ε), where λ is the growth rate, ε the error in the starting state and Δ the error that is regarded as useless. Because of the logarithm, improving the initial data by a factor of 10 gains only a fixed extra time (about 2.5 time units in the lab); a thousand times more precise data gains 7.6 units, which in the real weather is a few days at most."),
            ("Predictable and unpredictable", "Not all weather is equally unpredictable. Large systems such as depressions are predictable for 1–2 weeks. Small ones such as individual thunderstorms or tornadoes only for hours: errors grow faster on small scales, and these then affect larger scales. This is why a forecaster can say a front will arrive on Tuesday but not exactly which street will have the hail."),
            ("Order in chaos", "Chaotic systems are not formless. The Lorenz attractor has a definite shape, and the weather has a definite climate. This is why ensembles and probabilities work: a cloud of nearby starting points spreads out over the attractor, and the proportion of the runs that end up in a given region is the forecast probability."),
